<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CAPACITOR_DEBUG</key>
	<string>$(CAPACITOR_DEBUG)</string>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
        <string>InSight</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<!-- Google sign-in callback (D3: the only way an anonymous account can
	     be upgraded without losing its uid and history). The plugin's
	     GoogleAuthProviderHandler calls GIDSignIn.signIn(withPresenting:),
	     and GoogleSignIn-iOS hands the OAuth result back through a custom
	     URL scheme — the REVERSED_CLIENT_ID from GoogleService-Info.plist.
	     Without this block registered there is no scheme to return to.

	     The value cannot be committed: GoogleService-Info.plist is
	     untracked (it is account-gated, see docs/SHIP-CHECKLIST.md §4), so
	     this ships as a placeholder in the same REPLACE_WITH_* form as the
	     two files under web/.well-known/. `npm run check:store-copy`
	     fails while it is unreplaced — the failure is otherwise silent and
	     device-only: everything compiles, the account sheet opens, and the
	     flow simply never returns. -->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.cosaxo.insight.googlesignin</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>com.googleusercontent.apps.437999864865-vqpbjkbk34fsqms3st4pt09f72rc2dan</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>remote-notification</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Location, when-in-use only (D9). Shown verbatim in the system
	     prompt, so it says what actually happens rather than what the
	     feature is called — and since D84/D175 that is TWO things, not
	     one: the one-time on-device city match, and (only if the user
	     switches Near on) the ~200 m presence square that IS sent and
	     stored while Near runs. The string claimed "used once … never
	     sent anywhere" until 2026-08-20, which stopped being true the day
	     Near shipped; web/privacy.html's location section is the wording
	     this mirrors. -->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Used on this device to work out which city you are nearest, so the daily can show how your city answered — only the city name is kept, and you can pick your city from a list instead. If you switch on Near, a rough ~200-metre square worked out from your location is shared while Near is on, so the app can show how many people are around you. Your exact coordinates never leave this device.</string>
	<!-- The same string again, for an authorisation this app never asks for.
	     Apple's upload analysis reads the linked binary, not the call
	     sites: @capacitor/geolocation depends on ion-ios-geolocation, whose
	     IONGLOCAuthorisationRequestType has an `always` branch naming
	     CLLocationManager.requestAlwaysAuthorization, so that symbol ships
	     in every archive and the key becomes mandatory even though nothing
	     in this app can reach it. Without it, App Store Connect accepts the
	     build and then emails ITMS-90683 — which is how this was found, on
	     2.0.0 build 10.

	     Both halves verified in source rather than inferred from the
	     warning: the `always` branch is in the upstream enum, and the
	     plugin's only call site is
	     `requestLocationAuthorisation(type: .whenInUse)`
	     (GeolocationPlugin.swift). The plugin's own README says to add this
	     key with the same text as the when-in-use one, because the prompt
	     cannot appear.

	     Identical by rule: `npm run check:ios-location` fails if the two
	     strings drift. A string nobody can see is one nobody would notice
	     growing a claim the app does not honour — and this one sits beside
	     web/privacy.html's "no location history, no background location".
	     The key grants nothing on its own: an Always prompt needs an Always
	     request, and UIBackgroundModes above carries remote-notification
	     only. The same check holds that pair too. -->
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Used on this device to work out which city you are nearest, so the daily can show how your city answered — only the city name is kept, and you can pick your city from a list instead. If you switch on Near, a rough ~200-metre square worked out from your location is shared while Near is on, so the app can show how many people are around you. Your exact coordinates never leave this device.</string>
	<!-- PRECISE SINCE D175, and this key flipping is the whole of that
	     decision on iOS. It used to be <true/>, which asked for a
	     neighbourhood-level fix and showed "Precise: Off" in the permission
	     sheet, with the comment here promising that reduced was "not a
	     default we quietly escalate from". It is not being escalated
	     quietly: the owner asked for a venue-scale Near, a ~1 km fix cannot
	     honestly measure one, and the alternative was a finer grid computed
	     from a coarse measurement — precision invented rather than
	     measured, which this app refuses everywhere else.
	     What did NOT change: the coordinate is still folded to a grid cell
	     on the device and discarded (src/v2/data/locate.ts), so nothing
	     finer than the cell ever leaves the phone, and the cell is 0.002°
	     (~220 m) — deliberately one step COARSER than Apple's own "Precise
	     Location" threshold of three decimal places. The App Store label
	     still moves to Precise Location, because what the app REQUESTS is
	     what the label describes; docs/STORE-FORMS.md carries the row and
	     the reasoning. -->
	<key>NSLocationDefaultAccuracyReduced</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
